    <statement><p>A density function is given by <m>f(x)=\frac{1}{L}e^{-x/L}</m> for <m>x\geq 0\text{,}</m> where <m>L</m> is a positive constant. Find the mode of <m>f(x)\text{.}</m></p>
<p>Mode: <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>Since <m>L&gt;0\text{,}</m></p>
<p><me>\begin{aligned}
                    f'(x)
                    \amp = -\frac{1}{L^2}e^{-x/L}.
                    \end{aligned}</me></p>
<p>This is negative for every <m>x\geq 0\text{.}</m> Therefore, <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing on its domain, so the maximum occurs at the left endpoint. The mode is <m>x=0\text{.}</m></p>
</solution>
